WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.648
BRENDAN BURNS: Hi there.

00:00:17.648 --> 00:00:20.040
It's great to see
everybody out here.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.750
I'm excited to talk to you
about Kubernetes today.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:24.680
I'm Brendan Burns.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.475
I'm a software
engineer at Google.

00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:28.600
There's going to be a couple
other people coming up

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.810
over the course, and they'll
introduce themselves.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.500
So you heard a little bit about
Kubernetes in the keynote,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.670
and so hopefully if you
hadn't heard about it before,

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.150
you've got a little
bit of context.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.050
We're going to go a little
bit deeper today and try

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.370
and explain both where we're
coming from, why we built it,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.680
and what's the new awesomeness
that we just built.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.750
So I wanted to take a
little bit of a step

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.000
back and give you a little
bit of the history of where

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.700
we came from.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.300
Eric went into this a
little bit this morning,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.040
so we're going to kind of move
through it relatively quickly,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:57.998
so you don't get bored.

00:00:57.998 --> 00:01:00.450
Hopefully you saw some of
that stuff in the opening

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.620
keynote today.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.440
But, in some sense,
Google, a long time

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.820
ago, exactly like the
way a lot of enterprise

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.400
and a lot of other
data centers are now,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.490
every single app
had its own machine.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.180
You would use
ticketing and things

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.320
like that to find machines.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.760
It was hard to manage,
hard to scale, painful

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.450
for people to operate.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.890
And so we started
to figure out, well,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.500
what do we need to do in order
to move away from the sort

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.170
of one-app-per-machine model?

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.082
And the very beginnings of
that were things like chroots,

00:01:35.082 --> 00:01:37.540
using [? Nice, ?] using some
of the traditional things that

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.400
allow you to
multi-tenant things.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.350
But it wasn't sufficient, right?

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.110
Noisy neighbors are
a significant problem

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.987
in that world, where some
intern running a MapReduce

00:01:47.987 --> 00:01:50.070
can totally obliterate
your search infrastructure,

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.240
and that's just not an
acceptable way to run things.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.010
And so moving
forward, we started

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.790
to develop technology that
enabled us to have better

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.530
isolation of these processes.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.220
And that eventually led
to things like cgroups

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.850
that Google contributed
to the Linux kernel.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.350
And what we found eventually was
that actually these isolation

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.380
boundaries really
helped us maximize

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.270
the utilization of the
machines to the point where

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.050
our utilization of machines is
significantly higher than that

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.540
of people basically throughout
the rest of the industry.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.520
And it's not necessarily
because the people who

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.604
use the machines are smarter
about packing things,

00:02:27.604 --> 00:02:29.686
but it's actually just so
we have better isolation

00:02:29.686 --> 00:02:31.510
and that enables us
to practice for them.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.884
And that's really
what Kubernetes

00:02:32.884 --> 00:02:35.370
is trying to do as well.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.490
So then, eventually,
Docker came around

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.130
and took a lot of
these technologies

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.170
and packaged them up in
a really amazing way.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.176
And Eric said that the container
image that Docker came around

00:02:44.176 --> 00:02:45.800
with and the building
tools around that

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.350
really sort of opened up
the world of containers

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.880
and some of the
value of containers

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.000
on the node to
the broader world.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.370
And suddenly we saw this
explosion of interest

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.547
in containers.

00:02:55.547 --> 00:02:57.630
It was really gratifying,
I think, for a lot of us

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.910
because suddenly all of the
stuff that we had been talking

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.450
about and thinking
about internally,

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.770
we could share with
the outside world.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.517
And so the time was right.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.350
About a little under
two years ago, the time

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.740
was right to start sharing
some of the experiences we'd

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.167
had over the
proceeding eight years

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.750
and to start listening
as well, right?

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.930
We were starting to build
out the cloud platform,

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.320
and we knew that our
own internal experience

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.020
wasn't necessarily
the same as where

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.470
our users were coming from.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.600
And so we wanted to go forth
with this project and share

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.680
what we knew, but
also accept and build

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.860
a community around
where people were.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.110
And that led to the
development of Kubernetes.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.444
Kubernetes, it's an open source
container management system.

00:03:34.444 --> 00:03:36.485
Again, you heard about it
this morning from Eric,

00:03:36.485 --> 00:03:39.092
so I don't want to
belabor the point.

00:03:39.092 --> 00:03:41.300
And you, actually, hopefully,
saw the demo as well in

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.216
the keynote yesterday.

00:03:42.216 --> 00:03:44.440
It supports multiple
cloud environments.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.920
It supports on-prem
physical machines.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.660
It supports the
Raspberry Pi cluster

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.450
that I have in my closet.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.990
And it's 100% open source.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:54.314
It's written in Go.

00:03:54.314 --> 00:03:56.730
If you haven't checked out Go,
it's a pretty fun language.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.350
So that by itself is
worth checking out.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.860
And it's all available
out on GitHub.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.449
The core principles
of Kubernetes-- I

00:04:04.449 --> 00:04:05.740
think this is really important.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.281
When we were going
into it, we really

00:04:07.281 --> 00:04:10.860
wanted to talk about what
we are trying to accomplish

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:11.930
when we built Kubernetes.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:13.805
And part of what we were
trying to accomplish

00:04:13.805 --> 00:04:15.820
is that we knew that
people were going

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.250
to be using-- unlike in Google,
where everything is built

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.910
from source, people are going
to take off-the-shelf software.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.250
They're going to take MySQL.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:23.730
They're going to take Nginx.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.938
They're going to put them
in containers and run them.

00:04:25.938 --> 00:04:29.430
And rewriting those applications
just isn't an option for users.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.740
And then the other
important principle

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.640
it runs throughout Kubernetes is
that coupling is really, really

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:35.460
bad.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.170
When you take systems and you
package them together tightly

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:39.700
and so they're
tightly coupled, you

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.410
end up making changes
in one place that

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:44.100
break things in another place
that you weren't anticipating.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.980
And that leads to instability.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.945
It leads to an inability to
move your software forward--

00:04:48.945 --> 00:04:50.820
all of the things that
are necessary to build

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.774
a scalable, agile
platform that go away

00:04:54.774 --> 00:04:56.940
if you couple your software
really tightly together.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.080
So we really wanted to
decouple our software.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.190
And then there's a lot about
making sure that we are open.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.740
We really saw that this
open infrastructure

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.130
movement was happening.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.380
It was something that was
important to participate in.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.630
You're even starting to see
this on the hardware side,

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.870
on the data center design
side, with Facebook

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:19.000
and others participating in
open data center designs.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.500
It's just that's the way that
the data centers of the future

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.850
are going to be built, and
we wanted to be part of that.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.400
And then also, what
I like to say--

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.400
I don't know if people
have had a chance to take

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.990
a look at the Kubernetes paper
that we published recently.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:33.710
But one of the
key insights there

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:36.264
is that Kubernetes and
container-clustered

00:05:36.264 --> 00:05:37.680
orchestration
systems really allow

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.820
you to change the primary
key of your data center

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.600
from being the machine to
being the application, right?

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.430
We want people to start really
forgetting about machines

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.950
and thinking about
applications instead.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:54.430
And so all of this sort of
says we have experienced

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.860
a lot of the things that
are bothering people

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.960
right now out there in
software development.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.860
And we've built some
solutions that we really

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.270
believe help you move
on, and this is an image

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.130
that I use it a lot of my talks,
and I think it really captures,

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.140
in some sense, what we're
trying to accomplish.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.760
The challenge of
driving something

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:14.910
like this semi truck that
crashed in the middle

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:18.410
here is the semi truck is
a coupled system, right?

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.720
There is the cab that is
pulling it and the trailer.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.030
And as anybody's who's
backed up a trailer knows,

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.580
you can be backing
up in one direction,

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.620
and your trailer can
be going in completely

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.360
the opposite direction, right?

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.020
That coupling, that
unintended consequence

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.790
of some action-- because rarely
do you take actions that you

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.090
think are going to fail, right?

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:38.720
Rarely do you,
like, push a config

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.386
because you know that
it's bad, and it's

00:06:40.386 --> 00:06:43.080
going to cause your
application to go down.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.220
But the reason you have
all those failures related

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.260
to human error is that there's
an unanticipated consequence.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.500
And that unanticipated
consequence almost always

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.212
comes from this
kind of coupling.

00:06:56.212 --> 00:06:57.920
And to illustrate this
a little bit more,

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.250
when you think about things
in a machine-oriented way,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.277
when your data center
is machine-oriented

00:07:02.277 --> 00:07:04.110
and you think about
physical infrastructure,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:05.800
you end up with
things like this,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.360
where services are
splattered across three

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.740
different machines.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.910
And when you think
about the machine,

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.700
you think, oh, OK, I
really need to upgrade libC

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.120
on machine number one,
because my back end needs

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.490
this new version of libC.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.370
And so you go in and you
upgrade libC on machine one,

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.020
and your back end is
like, I'm awesome.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.019
I'm happy to go.

00:07:27.019 --> 00:07:28.560
And then it turns
out your front ends

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.032
all crashed because they
use that library too,

00:07:31.032 --> 00:07:32.740
but they use a slightly
different version

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.580
of that library, and
there's a bug in the library

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.996
that you just upgraded that
caused them to start crashing.

00:07:36.996 --> 00:07:38.780
That's the unintended
consequence, right?

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.917
That's why coupling
in this world is bad.

00:07:41.917 --> 00:07:43.500
And so what we would
really like to do

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.350
is show you how Kubernetes
enables you to create what

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.670
we call logical infrastructure.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.170
And logical
infrastructure basically

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.020
says, forget about
the machines, right?

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.080
The machines disappear
below the Kubernetes API,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.560
below this unified substrate.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.300
And you start thinking
about your applications.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.930
It's an application-oriented
infrastructure.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.520
All of your front
ends are the same.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.860
The thing that you're going
to change is your front end.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.235
The thing that you're going
to upgrade is your front end.

00:08:08.235 --> 00:08:10.690
And in this way, you've
decoupled all of these systems

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.200
from one another,
and you don't have

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.150
those unintended consequences
that cause stability problems.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.130
Another really important
part of being open and being

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.570
transparent is this idea of
workload portability, right?

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.570
There's all sorts of
reasons for people

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:27.900
to be in multiple
kinds of data centers,

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.800
be at their own
on-prem data centers,

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.169
private cloud, public
cloud, co-location,

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:33.351
whatever it happens to be.

00:08:33.351 --> 00:08:34.809
There's a lot of
reasons why you're

00:08:34.809 --> 00:08:36.289
going to be in a lot
of different places.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:38.580
And we profoundly believe
that the future of cloud

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:39.669
is hybrid, right?

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:43.559
And so in that world, it's
important to have a system that

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:45.830
spans these things
and enables you

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.600
to move your application from
vendor to vendor, if they're

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.821
having stability problems,
if they're having pricing

00:08:50.821 --> 00:08:52.570
that you don't like,
if a different vendor

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.403
comes along and has
better pricing, whatever

00:08:54.403 --> 00:08:56.260
the motivation happens to be.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.690
Not being locked into
a particular vendor

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.860
by using their APIs is
really powerful for you

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:02.700
as an end user.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.840
And Kubernetes, with its open
source aspect and its ability

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.120
to run anywhere, means
if you write applications

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.550
to the Kubernetes APIs, those
APIs are available and can

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.700
move with you wherever
you choose to go,

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:17.351
or indeed can normalize
your environment as well.

00:09:17.351 --> 00:09:19.100
And so this comes down
to the sort of idea

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.340
of write once, run
anywhere, which, by now,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.250
kind of has sort of
a bad reputation,

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.320
I guess, at some level
because, of course,

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.260
there's the asterisks, right?

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.340
This is mostly true.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:31.040
This isn't 100% true.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.780
But really, if you build in a
container, which encapsulates

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:36.920
all of its dependencies into
this hermetically sealed box

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.710
and you deploy and
you use services

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:41.300
provided by the
Kubernetes API, you really

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.360
are targeting a run
time that is pretty

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.920
independent of
the infrastructure

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.420
that it's running
on top of, right?

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.740
In the same way that if you
write a program in a higher

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.570
level language,
usually-- and that's

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.260
where the asterisk
comes in of course--

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.480
that program can run on a
variety of architectures,

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:04.160
from ARM in the Raspberry Pi,
to x86, to MIPS on a router,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.060
or whatever it happens to be.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.270
And some examples of this
inside the Kubernetes API,

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.234
where we've really try
to put in some effort

00:10:10.234 --> 00:10:11.650
into making these
things portable,

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.900
include things like our
networking model, where

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.850
we really tried to be agnostic.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:17.880
We didn't just sort of
say, like, oh, hey, this

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.250
works great on GCE.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.040
Nobody else can do
it anywhere else.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.940
Ingress is another
really great example

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:26.860
of this, where we created an
API object for doing HTTP load

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:27.400
balancing.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:29.830
And actually the implementation
of how that actually

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.380
produces a balancer,
is developed

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.590
outside of the Kubernetes
core code base.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.760
So if you want one for your
favorite cloud provider,

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:41.870
you want one for your physical
hardware load balancer,

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:43.340
you can build all
of these things.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.430
And there's one API object,
but the implementation

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.630
can be in a variety
of different ways.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:51.589
Our services are
examples of this as well,

00:10:51.589 --> 00:10:54.130
as well as persistent volumes,
where, I think, at this point,

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.610
we support 12 different volume
types, different distributed

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.510
storage technologies,
different cloud storage

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:00.360
technologies--
really enables you

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.485
to be isolated from the
specifics of the platform

00:11:03.485 --> 00:11:05.540
that you're running on.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.030
And then, finally, I talk
a lot about this coupling.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.714
We really think that
the Kubernetes API

00:11:10.714 --> 00:11:12.880
allows you to build these
systems that are decoupled

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.120
from each other so
that you can actually

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.780
have different teams doing
rollouts at different layers.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:20.767
Your front end team can roll
out a new set of the front ends

00:11:20.767 --> 00:11:23.350
without ever communicating with
the middleware or the back end

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:24.660
teams at all.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.500
Or the middleware
team can do a rollout

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.550
and never communicate with the
front end team or the back end

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:29.049
team.

00:11:29.049 --> 00:11:33.020
That's hugely important for
distributed teams, for scaling

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.630
teams up, and all
of these things

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:37.300
are made available
by the ability

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:43.000
to isolate things behind a
service IP that is unchanging,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:43.500
right?

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.830
It doesn't matter that
the actual pods that

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:49.460
implemented a particular service
have come up or gone down.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:53.950
The front end still just
talks to the same service IP.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.910
Likewise, we also want
you to be decoupled

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.730
from Kubernetes itself.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.710
We don't want to
provide tools so

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:02.320
that if you have a
configuration that

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.510
looks for a particular
environment variable,

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:07.520
you don't have to teach Nginx
that it's running inside

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:08.580
of Kubernetes.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.510
You can use things
like the downward API

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:13.100
to project parts of
the Kubernetes API

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.970
into environment variables
that Nginx already

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.160
knows how to consume, right?

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.368
This is part of meeting
people where they are as well

00:12:19.368 --> 00:12:23.250
as ensuring that that
application that somebody wrote

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:25.326
isn't a Kubernetes version
of the application.

00:12:25.326 --> 00:12:26.950
It's the same
application, whether it's

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:31.620
running inside of Kubernetes or
any other kind of environment.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:34.140
And then the other
part of this is

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.650
you can do this kind of
lift and shift, right?

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:40.480
Like maybe you're hybrid, but
you don't want to be stuck.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.450
If you get to a place where
the platform doesn't work,

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:48.120
if you're in GKE and you reach
that place where you're like,

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:53.020
no, we really need to be on-prem
for some reason, we'll be sad,

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:54.860
of course, but you
can do that, right?

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:57.690
You can take your
workload out of GKE,

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:02.482
roll it on over to
an on-prem cluster.

00:13:02.482 --> 00:13:04.940
Or likewise, if you're in a
different public cloud provider

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:07.210
right now-- we
understand lots of people

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:08.770
use different public clouds.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.250
You can start with Kubernetes
in that environment

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.740
and build a place
where, as we demonstrate

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.300
to the value of running inside
the Google Cloud platform,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.860
you'll be able to easily
move that application across,

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.800
or you can gradually
move your application

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.390
from one platform to another.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:29.500
And that's also a key component
of being open and being hybrid.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.919
So that was sort
of the prehistory.

00:13:31.919 --> 00:13:33.960
Hopefully it wasn't too
redundant with the things

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:35.310
that happened in the keynote.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.810
I'd like to take a step
forward to now, to what's

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.830
next, what's going on here.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.470
So one thing I really like to
highlight here is the velocity.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:46.390
And Eric sort of mentioned
this with a high level 27,000

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:46.890
commits.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.170
But this graph, I think,
really speaks to it

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.380
in a more dramatic fashion.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.310
We're doing 46 commits a day.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.011
And that's averaged
across the weekend,

00:13:55.011 --> 00:13:57.260
and we don't actually do
that many commits on weekends

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:59.590
because we all have lives.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:02.000
And so the velocity with
which we are doing this

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:02.930
is just amazing.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.430
And as well, at the
same time, we're

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.310
seeing new people come
into the community.

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:09.090
That's fantastic as well.

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:10.222
Really excited to see that.

00:14:10.222 --> 00:14:12.180
Really excited to see
all of the companies that

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.820
are contributing-- not just
using, but contributing

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:16.660
as well.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:19.420
We really want to build a
community that isn't just

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:21.590
a bunch of Googlers
throwing code over the wall

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:23.587
but is really a
holistic community.

00:14:23.587 --> 00:14:25.920
As part of that, you may have
also seen the announcement

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:30.820
that we've contributed
Kubernetes to the Cloud Native

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:31.710
Computing Foundation.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.626
So the intellectual
property around Kubernetes

00:14:33.626 --> 00:14:35.680
is now actually out and
available and available

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.070
to the Linux Foundation.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.310
And as part of that, I want to
introduce Rich Steinberg, who's

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:42.160
a principle engineer
at WePay, who's

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:45.420
going to talk a little bit
about how they use Kubernetes

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:47.910
to power WePay, so Rich?

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.316
RICH STEINBERG: Thanks, Brendan.

00:14:50.316 --> 00:14:55.540
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:58.140
Hi, I'm Rich, and
I'm going to talk

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:02.840
about WePay and our
experience with Kubernetes.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:06.000
So WePay, I know you're going
to find it hard to believe,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.060
but we're about payments.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.570
We do payments.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:13.620
We're startup down in
Redwood City, and what we do

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.490
is we do payments
between individuals.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.989
We allow our partners to
build the relationship

00:15:19.989 --> 00:15:21.530
between individuals,
and then we take

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:23.420
over the payment from there.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:26.610
And as part of that, we
hold credit card data,

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.550
and we're PCI certified.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.070
And we do this
using a PHP monolith

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:37.180
application that has been
changing the different business

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:37.680
models.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:40.230
And now that we're
taking off like crazy,

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.565
this PHP model needs
to be taken apart.

00:15:42.565 --> 00:15:45.740
It needs to be broken
into smaller services,

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:47.760
and we need a way to
deploy these services.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:51.600
So we took-- let
me move forward.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:54.330
So we took three servings
at this bat or this ball.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:58.990
The first was we had
some bad SQL that

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:01.730
was running against our database
and we needed to move that off.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:03.710
So we knew we
wanted to do Docker,

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.420
and we put this load
into a Docker container,

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:09.520
and we started it manually,
and we had it going.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:13.150
It wasn't great, but
it really helped.

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:16.290
The second was
Ansible and deploying

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:17.700
to individual machines.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:20.180
And we didn't really know how
to do topology at that point,

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:25.210
so it was really one
Docker container per VM,

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:28.200
and that was it.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:31.160
And we were in Google
Cloud, and Kubernetes,

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.220
we were part of the community.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:34.999
We were part of
the beta program.

00:16:34.999 --> 00:16:37.290
We had developers inside
WePay that were really excited

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:38.400
about Kubernetes.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:43.870
And as soon it became GA in
July of 2015, we were on board.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:46.410
And it really dramatically
changed our whole business,

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:48.470
because now we're able
to spread out workload

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:49.620
across multiple machines.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:51.110
It solved Canary deploys.

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:53.680
It solved being able
to rolling deployments.

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:56.439
It really just sort of changed
us, and it's been great.

00:16:56.439 --> 00:16:58.730
And one of things I want to
underscore about Kubernetes

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:02.000
is that it's changing rapidly.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.109
The problems and things that
we've been dealing with,

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:07.700
we can look at a roadmap map
and actually see features

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:08.800
that are going to help us.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:11.294
But more than that, it
actually is real use cases

00:17:11.294 --> 00:17:12.210
that we have problems.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:14.501
So I want to talk a little
bit about some of the things

00:17:14.501 --> 00:17:17.450
that we've done
with Kubernetes that

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:20.000
have been sort of outside
of what Kubernetes can do.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.270
And then we'll go from there.

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:26.119
So because we're PCI
certified-- and the keynote

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:30.390
talked a little bit about this,
about not having a GUI center--

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.430
everything that
communicates back and forth

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:33.180
must be encrypted.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.380
And that's just from the
early stages of Kubernetes

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:36.620
wasn't available.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:41.790
So what we did is built
a sidecar in the pod

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:46.220
with Nginx in it, and Kube
DNS gives you a domain name.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:47.680
It's like a private domain.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:51.760
And we built our
own in-house CA that

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.850
served that CN as
the domain name,

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:57.020
and that's how we
terminated SSL.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.962
Another example is since
we're PCI certified,

00:17:59.962 --> 00:18:01.420
when the PCI auditors
come in, they

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:03.950
want to look at the smallest
number of machines possible.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:06.330
They do not want to look at
hundreds and hundreds of VMs.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.800
They want to be basically
down to the smallest [? set ?]

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:09.490
possible.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:11.450
And in Kubernetes
terms, what that

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:15.030
means-- a dedicated
cluster for PCI.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:17.390
And with a dedicated
cluster for PCI,

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:19.330
we then need two
dedicated clusters,

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:22.050
because we need high
availability across zones,

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:25.440
and so then you have
four dedicated clusters.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.490
And these clusters need to
communicate with one another.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:31.430
So there's a need for some
sort of intercluster service

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:32.479
discovery.

00:18:32.479 --> 00:18:34.520
And what we're doing now
is we're jut hard coding

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:35.936
those service end points.

00:18:35.936 --> 00:18:37.560
But we're looking
forward to Ubernetes,

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:39.200
and we've also
looked at Buoyant.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:40.970
But Ubernetes is
really cool at being

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:44.390
able to solve this problem.

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:47.220
Lastly is Secrets,
and there's a lot

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:48.470
of talk about Secrets today.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.020
They talked about
in the keynote.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:54.206
And it's a big thing
you've got to consider.

00:18:54.206 --> 00:18:56.080
So the way we're taking
care it at this point

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:58.800
is using Hashicorp
Vault. And that's

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.770
just encryption as a
service, and there's

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:05.780
authenticated API calls that
are scoped to the application.

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:07.560
So the really good
thing about this

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:12.280
is that it's not putting Secrets
unencrypted anywhere on disk.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:14.090
It's memory-to-memory
communication,

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:17.110
and it's basically leased out.

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:20.670
So that's been sort
of some use cases.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:24.440
And I want to say again that
Kubernetes doesn't really

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:25.452
put you into a box.

00:19:25.452 --> 00:19:27.410
You can really kind of
do what you want with it

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:30.700
and take what they've given
us and build on top of it.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:33.920
And at the same time,
it's constantly changing.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:37.050
These problems are being
solved by the Kubernetes team.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:38.780
And I'm going to
hand it over to Tim

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:41.620
right now to tell
you about that.

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:46.900
TIM HOCKIN: Thanks, Rich.

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:47.780
Hi, everyone.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:48.280
I'm Tim.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:49.655
I'm one of the
software engineers

00:19:49.655 --> 00:19:50.590
working on Kubernetes.

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:51.800
So this is next.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:55.569
So let's talk about what's next.

00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:58.110
So I want to talk about a few
features that we're pushing out

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.625
in our Kubernetes 1.2 release
which was launched last week.

00:20:01.625 --> 00:20:03.000
And I want to talk
in some depth,

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:04.500
and I'm going to
do some live demos,

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:06.560
and we'll tempt the fates
and see what happens.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:08.670
So the first thing
that people experience

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.837
when they have these
distributed applications,

00:20:10.837 --> 00:20:13.420
they need to figure out how am
I'm going to update this thing?

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.567
This has been a topic at
a couple of the talks.

00:20:15.567 --> 00:20:17.150
I know Eric mentioned
it this morning.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:20.602
It was part of the demo
yesterday at the keynote.

00:20:20.602 --> 00:20:22.060
We're improving on
what we've done.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:25.630
So the goal here--
run an application,

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:27.920
update it with zero
downtime, and zero visibility

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.650
to your end users.

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:32.340
We built our rolling
update process completely

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:34.150
on our core primitives
of Kubernetes.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:35.110
There's nothing magic.

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:36.940
There's no back door APIs.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.370
If you don't like the rolling
update that we've built,

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:40.600
you can build your own.

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:42.740
This comes from a direct
lesson from [? Borg, ?]

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:44.940
where the update system was
really built and tightly

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:46.680
coupled into our system.

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:48.684
And it turns out
updating different kinds

00:20:48.684 --> 00:20:50.350
of applications,
there's different kinds

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:51.370
of requirements.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:57.010
So Version 1.0 and
1.1 of Kubernetes,

00:20:57.010 --> 00:21:00.770
we had this built into our cube
control or cube cuddle command

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:01.540
line.

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.750
In Version 1.2, we
have a new object

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:05.660
in our API called a Deployment.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:08.750
And a Deployment is
updates as a service.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.290
You tell the Deployment, I want
you to update from Version X

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.040
to Version Y, and you
take your hands off,

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:14.640
and the Deployment
object will do it.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:15.880
It's all done server side.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:17.880
You give it a bunch of
parameters that tells you

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:20.560
how fast you want to go and what
happens if there's a failure,

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.600
and you let it run.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.990
If you add this with
graceful termination of pods,

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:28.192
what you end up with is
a clean draining update

00:21:28.192 --> 00:21:28.900
from your system.

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.180
Any open connections, users
will finish their session,

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:35.515
and the old pod will go away,
and the new one will come up.

00:21:35.515 --> 00:21:38.570
I want to walk through a
little bit of the update.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:40.950
So imagine from what
you've got your service,

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:43.130
your wonderful MyApp.

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:46.400
And backing my application I've
got a replication controller

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.440
which has three pod instances
that are running it.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.390
This is the blue version
of the application.

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:54.380
If you notice here,
you'll see replicas three,

00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:58.424
and the selector is
MyApp Version 1, right?

00:21:58.424 --> 00:22:00.840
So the first thing you're going
to do in part of an update

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.215
is you're going to bring up
a new replication controller.

00:22:03.215 --> 00:22:06.450
Note specifically, this
is MyApp Version 2.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:07.950
Now this new
replication controller,

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.290
you're going to take it
from zero replicas to one.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:11.998
And now you've got a
new replica running.

00:22:11.998 --> 00:22:14.490
The service, if you'll notice
the selector on the service,

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:15.880
is app MyApp.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:16.980
It's version independent.

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.090
So at this point,
25% of my users

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:24.240
are going to be experiencing
my new application.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:25.980
This gives me a
really good signal,

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:29.210
if I can watch my monitoring,
that maybe my application is

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:31.350
bad and I want to roll it back.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.933
Well, let's assume that you got
it right, because you guys are

00:22:33.933 --> 00:22:35.316
all smart people.

00:22:35.316 --> 00:22:37.690
So you bring up a new one,
and you bring down an old one.

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:40.530
And you bring up a new one,
and you bring down an old one.

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.942
And you repeat this process
until the old version

00:22:42.942 --> 00:22:45.150
of the replication controller
has zero replicas left,

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.440
and then you can
just throw it away.

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:49.695
Like I said, this is
all done server side now

00:22:49.695 --> 00:22:51.370
in the Deployment
object, which you're

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.150
going to get for free if
you run cube cuddle run.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:59.350
It's new in the Version 1.2 API.

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:02.450
The next thing people tend
to run into is scaling.

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.480
I need my application
to be responsive.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:08.360
What happens if a
famous celebrity dies?

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.450
I need to be able to
respond with my application

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:11.830
in this change in load.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:14.040
So in the Kubernetes
1.2, we have

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:18.140
launched something we call
the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:19.080
That's a mouthful.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.200
But what it does is
it takes metrics.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:23.960
Currently, we do CPU
utilization or custom metrics,

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:26.260
which are alpha, but they work.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:29.000
You take these metrics. and you
feed it into the replication

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:30.960
system, and the
replication can say, hey,

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.110
you've crossed a threshold.

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:33.284
Let me scale you up.

00:23:33.284 --> 00:23:34.950
Or. hey, you've dropped
below threshold.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:36.949
Let me scale you down.

00:23:36.949 --> 00:23:38.740
Obviously, you give it
some min-max bounds,

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:40.240
and it operates
within those bounds.

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:42.840
But again, you set
it, and you forget it.

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:44.890
This frees you from
having to worry

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:47.680
about what happens if something
happens in a different time

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:50.150
zone and you're not at a
console and you aren't there

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:51.470
to provision new machines.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.300
This is the ethos of Kubernetes.

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:57.340
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:01.920
Next thing that people tend to
run into-- persistent storage,

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:02.530
right?

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:06.665
We'd like to talk about treating
your servers like cattle,

00:24:06.665 --> 00:24:07.720
right?

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:09.912
Everybody knows this analogy.

00:24:09.912 --> 00:24:10.870
Don't worry about them.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:11.275
They're fungible.

00:24:11.275 --> 00:24:13.050
If they go away,
it's not a big deal.

00:24:13.050 --> 00:24:15.600
But the reality is somewhere
at the bottom of your stack,

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:16.940
you've got a database.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:17.700
You've got files.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:20.720
You've got storage that
you need to run your stuff.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:24.180
So we have an abstraction
whole persistent volumes

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:26.330
in Kubernetes, which
lets you create

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:29.240
durable storage in
your network or on NFS

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:34.530
or in any one of almost two
dozen-- I'll correct Brendan.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:37.540
It's not 12; it's actually a
closer to 20 different volume

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:38.607
types.

00:24:38.607 --> 00:24:39.690
You store your data there.

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:43.190
And then as a user, you can
claim that data is yours.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:47.910
So new in Kubernetes 1.2,
we have auto provisioning.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.580
If you ask for data and there's
nothing in the system that

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.500
can satisfy that
request, we'll go off

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:53.810
and we'll make it for you.

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:56.018
Assuming you're in a cloud
provider that supports it,

00:24:56.018 --> 00:24:58.670
like GSE or Amazon
or OpenStack, we

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.149
can automatically go out and
make this new volume for you.

00:25:01.149 --> 00:25:03.440
What's really cool now is
you could write all your data

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:05.700
to this persistent
volume, and then you

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:07.440
can kill your
application, tear it down.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:08.023
You know what?

00:25:08.023 --> 00:25:11.000
My MySQL service is done, but
I need to offload all that data

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:12.220
into my monitoring system.

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:14.590
You can a hand off that
data to a different pod

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:17.100
because you still own that data.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.650
It exists until
you're done with it.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.490
We never destroy it until
you tell us that it's OK.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:26.150
This is an application-oriented
use of infrastructure.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:27.410
Don't think about the disks.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:28.770
Don't think about the iSCSIs.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:31.330
Don't think about where
they actually are.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:33.465
Think about what you
want to do with it.

00:25:33.465 --> 00:25:34.465
So let's do a live demo.

00:25:34.465 --> 00:25:35.923
If we can switch
to the demo, guys.

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:41.150
So I have to apologize.

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:43.810
First of all, I cannot type
and talk at the same time.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:45.390
So I have this self-typing demo.

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:48.990
But I promise you it is running
live against my GCE cluster

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.060
right now.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:52.260
So here we go.

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:59.880
So this script, the first
thing I'm going to show you,

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:00.840
there are no claims.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:02.950
PVC is a Persistent
Volume Claim.

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:04.434
It's a shortcut.

00:26:04.434 --> 00:26:06.350
I'm showing you with our
cube control command,

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:08.772
there are no claims
in our system.

00:26:08.772 --> 00:26:10.230
So let's go off
and create a claim.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:12.729
To do this, we're going to look
at this little blob of YAML.

00:26:12.729 --> 00:26:16.850
You can see here the claim is
called PD Provisioning Demo.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:20.110
It's existing in
the Demos namespace.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.300
What I'm asking for is something
that is read-write accessible,

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:25.070
and I want 10
gigabytes of space.

00:26:31.162 --> 00:26:32.870
And so just like that,
it's been created.

00:26:35.947 --> 00:26:37.780
And we're going to take
a look at what we've

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:39.240
done with the Describe command.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:44.880
And it shows you here that
we have a persistent volume

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:46.770
claim that is already
in the bound state.

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:49.340
What that means is it's
already gone off and created

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:51.590
a volume for us and
bound it to this claim.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:55.147
And it is satisfied
it with 10 gigabytes.

00:26:55.147 --> 00:26:56.980
So this loop is actually
going to do nothing

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:58.271
because it happened so quickly.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.730
So here you can
see the short form.

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:08.684
It happened 33 seconds ago.

00:27:08.684 --> 00:27:09.975
That's my proof that it's real.

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.480
So here, I ran
gcloud compute disks

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:17.839
list, which shows me in
the Google Compute Engine,

00:27:17.839 --> 00:27:18.880
here's the list of disks.

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.380
I grabbed for the ones that I
know that it's going to match,

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:23.940
and it's going to match
this dynamic disk.

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:26.200
So now I'm going to use this.

00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:29.020
Again, here's a little blob
of YAML that shows me a pod.

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:31.930
The important part here
is what the pod does.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:35.130
It does an sh -c, which says
go run this shell command.

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:39.060
It's going to touch a file
called /pv/ whatever my current

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:39.980
host name is.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:42.010
We set the host name
to the pod name,

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:44.580
so you can see that this
file gets created on the pod,

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.246
and then it's just
going to go to sleep.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:56.575
And just like that,
my pod is created,

00:27:56.575 --> 00:27:57.950
and it's probably
already running

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:01.320
before I can finish
typing it out.

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:02.570
So you can see it's scheduled.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.730
It's been assigned
to Minion I89N.

00:28:08.025 --> 00:28:10.756
And we'll wait for it to give
us a running status so that we

00:28:10.756 --> 00:28:11.880
can actually go poke at it.

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.480
OK, it is running.

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:21.700
So I'm going to use
to kubectl and exec

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.690
into this to give me a shell.

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:28.750
So I'm now live into
this running container.

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:34.020
If I ls in the /pv directory,
you see that this file is demo

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.270
blah-blah-blah.

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:36.160
That is it.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:51.570
So I'm going to say-- so
I've now created this file.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:53.967
So I'm going to exit the shell.

00:28:53.967 --> 00:28:56.550
And then I'm going go ahead, and
I'm going to delete that pod.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:58.133
Now this pod has
gone from the system.

00:28:58.133 --> 00:29:07.000
That running process is
gone, just to prove it.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:08.240
But my claim still exists.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.805
I still own this data.

00:29:10.805 --> 00:29:14.600
And it is still there,
and it is still bound.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:20.440
And in Compute Engine-- maybe.

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:21.140
Oh, there we go.

00:29:21.140 --> 00:29:22.159
It still exists.

00:29:22.159 --> 00:29:23.950
So now I'm going to
run another pod, right?

00:29:23.950 --> 00:29:25.170
My MySQL finished.

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:27.930
I want to go run
the post processing.

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:30.482
I want to analyze my
user logs or something

00:29:30.482 --> 00:29:31.440
that I want to do here.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:34.215
So I've gone and
created another pod.

00:29:34.215 --> 00:29:35.965
And it's going to check
that it's running.

00:29:44.409 --> 00:29:46.200
And I'm going to shell
into this one again.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:47.941
And what you're
going to see-- cross

00:29:47.941 --> 00:29:49.690
your fingers-- is that
those files that we

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:50.731
wrote before still exist.

00:29:54.636 --> 00:29:55.510
So there you can see.

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:58.070
Now I've got two files that
are specific to the host

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:00.200
name and the Hello file
that I wrote before.

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:01.520
Now, I can do this
any number of times.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:03.394
I can keep handing off
the pod from container

00:30:03.394 --> 00:30:05.980
to container-- or the volume
from container to container

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:07.340
until I'm done with it.

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:10.500
So let me exit this, and
say I'm done with it.

00:30:12.561 --> 00:30:13.935
So I'm going to
go delete my pod.

00:30:17.828 --> 00:30:20.705
And you can see again
the pod is gone.

00:30:20.705 --> 00:30:21.830
This is all happening real.

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:25.520
This is how fast the
system actually operates.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:31.600
I've deleted my claim,
and my claim is gone.

00:30:34.252 --> 00:30:36.460
And the persistent volume
that was backing that claim

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:37.160
is also gone.

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:39.650
And if I were to look in gcloud
which I don't think I did,

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:41.100
if I were to look at
gclould one more time,

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:42.380
it would show me that
that persistent volume

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:43.410
has been erased.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:45.000
What this means
for you is you are

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.500
free to work on your application
and not worry about who's

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:50.935
managing the infrastructure,
not send a ticket to provision

00:30:50.935 --> 00:30:51.640
a new volume.

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:53.389
Can you switch back
to the slides, please?

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:59.011
Demo number one-- it worked.

00:30:59.011 --> 00:31:04.040
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:07.550
I'm going to tempt the
fates and do it twice.

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:12.170
So new also in Kubernetes
1.2-- multi-zone clusters.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.100
You guys, you were asked, and
we heard, and we answered.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:17.200
So you can now
bring up a cluster

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:21.740
that spans multiple zones,
GCE zones or multiple Amazon

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:23.360
availability zones.

00:31:23.360 --> 00:31:25.826
We'll label the nodes that
come up on those zones.

00:31:25.826 --> 00:31:27.200
And when you
schedule work, we're

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:28.900
going to spread them
across those zones.

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:31.960
Now, if a zone blows
up, you're safe.

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:33.680
Your application's
not going to go down.

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:37.276
Kubernetes will say, oh, damn,
what happened to those copies?

00:31:37.276 --> 00:31:39.400
And it will bring up new
ones on these other nodes.

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.650
This is what it's all about.

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:44.060
2:00 AM, Sunday night,
you're sleeping.

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.430
Do you want your pager
to go off because Google

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:48.380
brought a zone down?

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:50.452
Heck no, just handle
it for me, right?

00:31:50.452 --> 00:31:51.660
Send me email in the morning.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:52.680
I'll take a look.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:53.700
This is what it's about.

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:55.930
So there's zero changes
to the Kubernetes API.

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:57.405
You don't need to
know about zones.

00:31:57.405 --> 00:31:58.780
All you need to
know is that when

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:00.780
you provision this cluster,
you've brought it up

00:32:00.780 --> 00:32:02.680
in multiple zones.

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:06.060
This is GA in Version 1.2.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.435
The code name for this project
has been called Ubernete.

00:32:09.435 --> 00:32:11.310
And Ubernetes is going
to continue to evolve.

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.351
We're going to build out
a more federated cluster

00:32:13.351 --> 00:32:15.140
model in upcoming
versions of our system.

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:16.790
This is what we
call Ubernetes Lite.

00:32:18.972 --> 00:32:20.680
So the next thing that
people talk about,

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:24.320
and Brendan mentioned this--
Ingress, L7 load balancing.

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:28.185
We built the core of Kubernetes
around the idea of L3 and L4

00:32:28.185 --> 00:32:30.060
load balancing because
we wanted to be really

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:31.800
agnostic to applications.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.820
It turns out, unsurprisingly,
a lot of your building

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:36.520
HTTP-based applications.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:40.570
And you want a way to get
HTTP into your clusters.

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:42.970
So, again, you asked,
and we listened.

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:45.470
We built a new API
object called Ingress.

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:47.610
Ingress will allow
you to describe

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:51.960
a map based on host headers
or based on URL paths that

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.310
will take incoming
HTTP traffic and route

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:56.399
it to various Kubernetes
service backends.

00:32:56.399 --> 00:32:58.440
So now, again, you can
build much micro services.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:02.380
You could have your contact
manager as one application,

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:05.070
and you can have your email
box as another application,

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:07.876
and they can come in
through the same URL

00:33:07.876 --> 00:33:10.480
and be spread out into
these different services.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:13.720
This is literally
how Google operates.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:18.540
We have implementations of
the Ingress API against GCE.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.070
There's one against
Amazon that's

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:22.740
being developed right
now-- HA Proxy and Inginx.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.434
What this really means is if
you want to run on-premise,

00:33:25.434 --> 00:33:26.350
you can totally do it.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:28.166
You can run HA Proxy
on your premise.

00:33:28.166 --> 00:33:29.540
You can pick up
your application.

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:32.250
You can move it to GCE, and you
can get all the awesome power

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:37.470
of the GCE load balancer API,
if you saw that talk yesterday.

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:39.460
The reality of these
cloud provider APIs

00:33:39.460 --> 00:33:41.060
is sometimes
they're complicated.

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:44.090
And if you learn the
GCE load balancer API

00:33:44.090 --> 00:33:45.590
and then you want
to move to Amazon,

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.870
then you have to learn the
Amazon load balancer API.

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:50.359
And these two things can't
be any more different.

00:33:50.359 --> 00:33:51.900
And you don't want
to deal with that.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:52.750
That's a mess.

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:53.590
That's coupling.

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:56.340
So Ingress gives you a
way to couple from that.

00:33:59.930 --> 00:34:01.460
The next thing
people talk about,

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:05.520
and Rich mentioned this with
respect to his stuff at WePay,

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:06.980
is Secrets.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:09.400
You've got to authenticate
your applications.

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:12.251
You've got to give them
access to a secured something.

00:34:12.251 --> 00:34:13.250
I don't care what it is.

00:34:13.250 --> 00:34:14.310
Maybe it's an NSH key.

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:15.949
Maybe it's an API token.

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:20.650
Maybe it's a password
in plain text in a file.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:22.780
You have a need to
access these things.

00:34:22.780 --> 00:34:25.960
But we've all read the
12-Factor Manifesto, right?

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:28.940
12 Factor says, you
put your configuration

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:30.440
in the environment.

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:31.960
Well, Kubernetes
is the environment.

00:34:31.960 --> 00:34:33.500
This is where you're
getting stuff.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:37.449
So we are now giving you an
API to manage your Secrets

00:34:37.449 --> 00:34:38.989
through our unified
Kubernetes API

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:41.401
through the same cube control
command line interface,

00:34:41.401 --> 00:34:43.650
through the same YAML objects,
with the same semantics

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:45.330
as everything else.

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:48.630
You inject them into your
application late bound.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:49.959
You write it in you API.

00:34:49.959 --> 00:34:51.000
You run your application.

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:54.219
You join these two things
at the very last second.

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:56.610
They are written in a tmpfs.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:58.280
So you never write to the desk.

00:34:58.280 --> 00:34:59.940
They're only in memory.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:03.420
And you can then access them as
files or environment variables

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:05.320
from your application.

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:09.830
So let's do a live demo.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:11.800
Can we switch to demo screen?

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:13.035
Thank you.

00:35:13.035 --> 00:35:13.535
All right.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:24.040
So like everything else, there's
a little bit of YAML behind it.

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:26.080
I'm going to go off
and create a Secret.

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:28.220
It is called My Secret Password.

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:29.290
It is a type opaque.

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:31.890
It's not in TLS
certificate or anything

00:35:31.890 --> 00:35:33.610
that we might know about.

00:35:33.610 --> 00:35:36.370
The username and password
are [? Bay64 ?] here,

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:39.160
simply so we can stick binary
data into this YAML file.

00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:48.580
Like everything else, we
just run kubectl create -f.

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:51.544
It goes off and
creates my Secret.

00:35:51.544 --> 00:35:53.210
Now I'm going to go
of and create a pod.

00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:55.510
Again, all this pod
does is go to sleep,

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:57.390
but it mounts this Secret.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:59.530
You see the volume Secret here?

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:02.260
It's going to mount it
at this /data directory.

00:36:06.354 --> 00:36:07.520
So I'll just create the pod.

00:36:12.775 --> 00:36:14.400
And the pod is there,
and it's running.

00:36:17.950 --> 00:36:19.980
And again, I'm going
to shell into this

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:23.610
just to show you what we've got.

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:26.760
So if I look in /data now, I've
got this virtual directory that

00:36:26.760 --> 00:36:28.220
is password and username.

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:35.399
So I can cat slash data, and
you can see that it's me.

00:36:35.399 --> 00:36:36.690
And I can show you my password.

00:36:40.990 --> 00:36:43.960
And when I log out of
that pod, that's it.

00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:45.080
My pod's now running.

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:46.650
If that was an SSH
key, I could then

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:51.170
use that credentials to
get into the other peers,

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:53.560
or I could use it as an
API token to go into Twilio

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:55.540
or whatever I needed it to be.

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:58.500
You can switch back to
the main slides now.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:00.805
Demo number two!

00:37:00.805 --> 00:37:04.685
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:10.545
So I've only got a
few minutes left.

00:37:10.545 --> 00:37:13.120
And I wanted to talk about
some of the things we've done

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:17.600
and are doing around performance
and scalability of Kubernetes.

00:37:17.600 --> 00:37:22.360
We started 1.0, and we said
Kubernetes runs on 100 nodes.

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:25.260
And 100's a kind of
underwhelming number, right?

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:27.427
People said, Google, what
are you doing-- 100 nodes?

00:37:27.427 --> 00:37:29.801
And the truth is, I'm going
to look out in this audience.

00:37:29.801 --> 00:37:30.890
I'm not going do a survey.

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:33.150
But how many of you--
think in your head.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:36.150
How many of you have more than
100 serving nodes right now?

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:41.660
I don't need to see hands.

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:43.550
It's not that many people.

00:37:43.550 --> 00:37:44.140
But we heard.

00:37:44.140 --> 00:37:47.020
People said, actually, there
are enough people out there

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:48.020
that need more than 100.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:49.853
Then we decided we were
going to ramp it up.

00:37:49.853 --> 00:37:52.531
So the first thing we did is we
defined an SLO, a Service Level

00:37:52.531 --> 00:37:53.030
Objective.

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:55.000
And we said, we're not
going to ramp it up

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:56.980
unless we can do it fast, right?

00:37:56.980 --> 00:38:01.460
So we set our objective 99%
of all of our API calls return

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:04.140
in less than one
second, and 99% of pods

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:05.950
start up in less
than five seconds.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.470
And that has to be true on
a fully loaded cluster--

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:10.930
the first pod and the last pod.

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:13.960
And then we use that to
test how high we can go.

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:15.670
In Kubernetes 1.2,
I'm happy to say,

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:20.240
we're doing more than 1,000
nodes at more than 30,000 pods

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:24.080
in a given cluster, with our
API server meeting this SLO.

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:25.890
Now, if you don't
mind about the SLO,

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:28.760
you can probably double or
even triple those numbers,

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:31.330
and things will get
a little bit slower.

00:38:31.330 --> 00:38:33.624
But this is where we are
with 1.2-- 1,000 nodes.

00:38:33.624 --> 00:38:35.290
Now, all those people
who thought, yeah,

00:38:35.290 --> 00:38:38.530
I have more than 100 nodes, do
you have more than 1,000 nodes?

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:41.000
I bet there's a very,
very small number of you

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:42.890
out there, which is fine.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:46.090
When you need it, when
your startup hits at gold,

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:48.024
we're there for you.

00:38:48.024 --> 00:38:49.440
Other things we've
done in the 1.2

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.190
release-- we revamped
our cube proxy

00:38:52.190 --> 00:38:56.970
system, which is the backbone of
our virtual IP services model.

00:38:56.970 --> 00:39:00.289
In the 1.0 and 1.1 release,
it did a user space copy,

00:39:00.289 --> 00:39:01.830
which we knew was
a little bit slower

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:03.663
and was going to be
limiting to scalability.

00:39:03.663 --> 00:39:05.550
In 1.2, it never
moves to user space.

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:07.440
It's purely done in kernel.

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:08.980
Man, is it fast.

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:11.790
For the demo that
we did the keynote,

00:39:11.790 --> 00:39:13.300
we were getting
ready to get set up,

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:14.758
and we were realizing
that the load

00:39:14.758 --> 00:39:16.210
testers were causing problems.

00:39:16.210 --> 00:39:17.940
And the first thing
that we did was

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.070
we switched to the
IP tables proxy,

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.350
and all the problems were gone.

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:27.400
So if I didn't believe
it before, that's it.

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:32.450
Additionally, in 1.2 massive
reduction in the usage of CPU

00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:34.630
and memory that our own
components use on the system

00:39:34.630 --> 00:39:38.780
this for x number comes
from the guy's Red Hat.

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:41.060
So don't take my word for it.

00:39:41.060 --> 00:39:43.120
This means there's
more memory and CPU

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:45.230
left for you to run
your applications.

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:48.830
We want to be as slim as
we possibly can be for you.

00:39:48.830 --> 00:39:52.200
Now, this, of course, isn't
good enough, so what's next?

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:55.120
1.3-- we're planning
to massively increase

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:56.410
the scale again.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:59.930
I don't want to talk numbers
just yet, but it'll be big.

00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:03.130
We're talking about moving
to a binary encoded protocol.

00:40:03.130 --> 00:40:06.030
JSON, it turns
out, kind of slow.

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:07.830
When we ran a profile
against our system,

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:11.054
the number one hit was
JSON marshal and unmarshal.

00:40:11.054 --> 00:40:13.220
So we're going to move to
a binary encoded protocol.

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:15.610
You'd still be JSON
there if you wanted,

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:18.290
but our
component-to-component stuff

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:22.070
will all be done through
[? Protoba ?] and GRPC.

00:40:22.070 --> 00:40:25.322
We also are adding a bunch of
caching and parallelization

00:40:25.322 --> 00:40:26.030
in our scheduler.

00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:28.238
So our scheduler throughput
will be massively faster.

00:40:28.238 --> 00:40:30.465
It will be massively
more scalable.

00:40:30.465 --> 00:40:34.341
Then you can see here our graph
from our current performance

00:40:34.341 --> 00:40:34.840
metrics.

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:36.632
We're publishing a
blog post on this.

00:40:36.632 --> 00:40:37.840
I'm not sure if it's out yet.

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:38.710
But it'll be soon.

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:40.709
And you'll be able to
read a lot more about what

00:40:40.709 --> 00:40:45.620
we've done with performance and
scalability on Kubernetes 1.2.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:47.820
All of this is
available right now.

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:50.030
Kubernetes 1.2 is in the
middle of rolling out

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:51.430
to GKE right now.

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:53.784
In fact, it might be
done this very day.

00:40:53.784 --> 00:40:55.950
If you want it, you can go
to your container engine.

00:40:55.950 --> 00:40:58.050
You can run the upgrade
command, and you

00:40:58.050 --> 00:41:02.276
can get all this goodness
right here, right now.

00:41:02.276 --> 00:41:06.228
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:13.430
So, all that said, we
have a ton of work to do.

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:17.690
We have 800 man years
of work on our roadmap.

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:19.900
I'm not even kidding,
and that's if we only

00:41:19.900 --> 00:41:21.710
do the things we know about.

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.150
Kubernetes is an open project.

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:24.890
We built up from
the very beginning

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:29.820
to be open source, open ideas,
open standards, open community.

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:31.850
We do everything in the public.

00:41:31.850 --> 00:41:35.910
If anybody here has clicked
that Watch icon in GitHub

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:38.340
on the Kubernetes repo,
I feel bad for you,

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:41.880
because there is
so much going on.

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:42.760
It's exciting.

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:45.120
I'm passionate and
excited about this.

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:46.000
We need your help.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:47.860
We need people who
are using this thing

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:49.920
to come in and
tell us what works,

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:52.170
what doesn't work, what do
you need out of the system,

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:53.430
because we are listening.

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:56.280
We want to make it
work for you guys.

00:41:56.280 --> 00:41:57.970
So you can see here,
we've got our URL.

00:41:57.970 --> 00:41:59.820
The code, of courses,
is on GitHub.

00:41:59.820 --> 00:42:02.120
All of us hang out on
Slack all the time.

00:42:02.120 --> 00:42:03.650
Come and ask us questions.

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:05.320
We have dedicated
people who sit there

00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:07.870
and answer user support
questions all day.

00:42:07.870 --> 00:42:10.820
We're also on Stack Overflow,
and we're on Twitter.

00:42:10.820 --> 00:42:13.300
You can find Brendan and
myself and many other people

00:42:13.300 --> 00:42:15.730
there, and come and
ask us questions.

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:20.056
So I understand we are not
doing it to a Q&amp;A session now.

00:42:20.056 --> 00:42:21.430
But at the end of
these sessions,

00:42:21.430 --> 00:42:23.620
we will all be hanging
out in the Playground.

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:25.870
We'll grab a quick bite to
eat, and we'll be out there

00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:27.061
to do Q&amp;A, talk to people.

00:42:27.061 --> 00:42:28.060
Come tell us your story.

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:30.010
Tell us what worked,
what didn't work.

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:31.600
I want to hear from you.

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:33.300
So thank you all very much.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:36.650
[APPLAUSE]

